  "worked_solution": "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x-1} & <{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({5}^{x-1}) & <\\operatorname{ln}\u2061[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2-x}] \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 & <(2-x)(\\operatorname{ln}\u20611-\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 & <(2-x)(0-\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 & <(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 & <x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & <\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & <\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
